Day 7 – Change an EC2 Instance Type (AWS)

Objective

Optimize resource usage by changing the instance type of nautilus-ec2 from t2.micro to t2.nano.

Configuration
Setting                 Value
Instance Name           nautilus-ec2
Instance ID             i-0a2b5906251cd8c00
Availability Zone       us-east-1d
Original Instance Type  t2.micro
New Instance Type       t2.nano
On-Demand Linux price   t2.micro 0.0116 USD/hour, t2.nano 0.0058 USD/hour

Steps Performed
1. Opened the EC2 Console and selected the instance nautilus-ec2 (Stopped).
2. Opened Actions -> Instance settings -> Change instance type.
3. Confirmed the current instance type was t2.micro.
4. Selected t2.nano as the new instance type. The console showed the price comparison.
5. Applied the change.
6. Started the instance from Instance state. It moved from Stopped to Running, with status checks Initializing.

Important Concepts Learned
- Changing an instance type requires a stop: The instance must be stopped before its type can be changed. Stopping and starting also can change the public IP unless an Elastic IP is attached.
- Instance type sizing: t2.nano has 1 vCPU and 0.5 GiB memory. t2.micro has 1 vCPU and 1 GiB memory. Right-sizing reduces cost for workloads that do not need the extra memory.
- Compatibility: The new type must match the architecture (x86 or ARM) of the AMI. The console only offers compatible types.
- Burstable (T-series) instances: They use CPU credits. Running out of credits limits CPU performance.
- Data retention: Instance store data is lost when an instance is stopped, but EBS-backed root volumes are kept.

Key Takeaway
Changing the instance type is a stop-change-start operation. Choose the smallest type that meets the workload, because the cost difference is shown in the console before you change it.
